1
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:06,000
"I just want to be perfect."

2
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:06,000
Black Swan is a drama, thriller, horror film directed by Darren Aronofsky.

3
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:06,000
A committed dancer struggles to maintain her sanity after winning the
lead role in a production of Tchaikovsky's "Swan Lake".

4
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:06,000
Nina Sayers / The Swan Queen, played by Natalie Portman.

5
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:06,000
Lily / The Black Swan, played by Mila Kunis.

6
00:24:00,000 --> 00:24:06,000
Thomas Leroy / The Gentleman, played by Vincent Cassel.

7
00:28:00,000 --> 00:28:06,000
Erica Sayers / The Queen, played by Barbara Hershey.

8
00:32:00,000 --> 00:32:06,000
Beth Macintyre / The Dying Swan, played by Winona Ryder.

9
00:36:00,000 --> 00:36:06,000
David Moreau / The Prince, played by Benjamin Millepied.

10
00:40:00,000 --> 00:40:06,000
Black Swan is a 2010 American psychological horror thriller film directed by Darren Aronofsky from a
screenplay by Mark Heyman, John McLaughlin, and Andres Heinz, based on a story by Heinz.

11
00:44:00,000 --> 00:44:06,000
The film stars Natalie Portman in the lead role, with Vincent Cassel, Mila
Kunis, Barbara Hershey, and Winona Ryder in supporting roles.

12
00:48:00,000 --> 00:48:06,000
The plot revolves around a production of Tchaikovsky's Swan
Lake by the company of New York City Ballet.

13
00:52:00,000 --> 00:52:06,000
The production requires a ballerina to play the innocent and fragile White Swan, for which the committed dancer Nina Sayers (Portman) is a
perfect fit, as well as the dark and sensual Black Swan, which are qualities better embodied by the new rival Lily (Kunis).

14
00:56:00,000 --> 00:56:06,000
Nina is overwhelmed by a feeling of immense pressure when she finds herself competing for the
role, causing her to lose her tenuous grip on reality and descend into madness.

15
01:00:00,000 --> 01:00:06,000
Aronofsky conceived the premise by connecting his viewings of a production of Swan Lake with an unrealized screenplay about
understudies and the notion of being haunted by a double, similar to the folklore surrounding doppelgängers.

16
01:04:00,000 --> 01:04:06,000
Aronofsky cites Fyodor Dostoevsky's The Double as
another inspiration for the film.

17
01:08:00,000 --> 01:08:06,000
Aronofsky also considered Black Swan a companion piece to his film The Wrestler (2008), with
both films revolving around demanding performances for different kinds of art.

18
01:12:00,000 --> 01:12:06,000
He and Portman first discussed the project in 2000, and after a brief attachment to Universal
Pictures, Black Swan was produced in New York City in late 2009 by Fox Searchlight Pictures.

19
01:16:00,000 --> 01:16:06,000
Portman and Kunis trained in ballet for several months prior to filming.

20
01:20:00,000 --> 01:20:06,000
Black Swan premiered at the 67th Venice International Film Festival on September 1, 2010, and had a limited
release in the United States starting on December 3, before opening in wide release on December 17.

21
01:24:00,000 --> 01:24:06,000
Upon release, the film received positive reviews from critics, with praise toward
Aronofsky's direction and the performances of Portman, Kunis, and Hershey.

22
01:28:00,000 --> 01:28:06,000
It also emerged as a major commercial success at the box-office,
grossing $330.3 million worldwide on a $13 million budget.

23
01:32:00,000 --> 01:32:06,000
The film received five nominations at the 83rd Academy Awards, including Best Picture and Best Director (Aronofsky), with Portman winning Best Actress; it also received four
nominations at the 68th Golden Globe Awards, including Best Motion Picture – Drama and Best Director (Aronofsky), with Portman winning Best Actress in a Motion Picture – Drama.

24
01:36:00,000 --> 01:36:06,000
In 2021, Portman's performance was included in The New Yorker's list
of the best film performances of the 21st century.

25
01:40:00,000 --> 01:40:06,000
The film explores themes of new york city,
dancing, dancer, nightmare, competition.

26
01:44:00,000 --> 01:44:06,000
The tone is intense and dark and emotional.
